es  well let's first think about p1  so pressure at time 1 is what it's 765  torr 765  torr and what's p2 that's the pressure  just before it burst and they tell us  it's 6.51 torr much lower pressure which  makes intuitive sense we're at a higher  altitude  6.51  torr now what is  v1  well they tell us that right over there  that is 1.85  times 10 to the third liters now what is  v2  well that's what they want us to figure  out what is the volume of the balloon  just before it bursts so i'll put a  little question mark there and then last  but not least what is t1  well they tell us the starting  temperature is at 23 degrees celsius but  you have to think on more of an absolute  scale and deal with temperatures in  terms of kelvin so to convert 23 degrees  celsius into kelvin you have to add 273.  so this is going to be 296  kelvin and then what is t2  well t2 is negative 44 degrees celsius  if we add 273 to that let's see that's  going to be if we subtract it's going to  be in my head 229  kelvin  and so we have everything we need in  order to solve for v2 in fact we could  solve for v2 before we even put in these  numbers if we multiply  both sides of this equation times t2  over p2 and the reason why i'm  multiplying it times this is so that  this cancels with this this cancels with  that so i have just v2 on the right hand  side of course i have to do that on both  sides t2  over p2 i am going to get and i'll now  color code it i'm going to get that t2  times  p1  times v1  over  p2  times t1  t  1  is equal to  v 2  v 2. so we just have to calculate this  right now let me give myself a little  bit more  real estate with which to do it and so  we could write that v2 is equal to t2  which is  229 kelvin times p1  which is  765  torr  times v1  which is 1.85  times 10 to the third liters  all of that  over  p2  which is 6.51  torr  times t1 which is 296  kelvin and we can confirm that the units  work out tor cancels with tor kelvin  cancels with kelvin so we're just going  to have a bunch of numbers a calculation  and our the units we're left with is  liters which is good because that is  what we care about when we care about  volume so this is going to be equal to  229  times 765  times 1.85  times  10 to the third  divided by 6.51  divided by 296 is equal to this business  right over here let's see we have three  significant digits here three  significant digits here three here three  here and three there so our answer is  going to have three significant figures  so it's going to be if we round it's  going to be 168 000 and so we could just  write that as 168 000 liters or if we  want to write that in scientific  notation we could write that as 1.68  times 10 to the 1 2 three four five  so let me write it that way so this is  going to be equal to 1.68  times 10 to the fifth  liters and i always like to do a nice  intuition check does that make sense so  our starting volume was  1850 liters and then our volume got a  lot larger because we're going to a much  higher altitude and that does make  intuitive sense to me
 

